# Target B component structure

Let `Theta` be the terminal graph. Its connected components are exact convex
Minkowski summands of the hull:

\[
\boxed{K=\sum_CK_C}
\]

up to translation.

For terminal chain `j`,

\[
L_j=
\operatorname{span}\{J(u_\tau-u_{\rho(j)}):\partial_{j\tau}\ne0\},
\qquad
t_j=\dim L_j,
\]

and for terminal component `C`,

\[
s_C=\dim\operatorname{span}_{j\in J_C}L_j.
\]

The componentwise closure theorem is

\[
\boxed{
|E(C)|-|V(C)|+1
\le
\sum_{j\in J_C}t_j-s_C.
}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
T-q+c(\Theta)
\le
\sum_jt_j-\sum_Cs_C.
}
\]

The equality classification is exact:

\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{ll}
s_C=0&\Rightarrow C\text{ is a one-direction tree};\\[1mm]
s_C=1,\ C\text{ cyclic}&\Rightarrow C\text{ uses exactly two train directions};\\[1mm]
s_C=2&\Rightarrow C\text{ has genuine two-dimensional phase turning}.
\end{array}
}
\]

A cyclic component that pays only one closure is therefore an exact two-family
ordered/Ferrers system.

---

# Target B component rotations: isolated but not free

For an active diameter bar `h`, the common component rotations satisfy the exact
stochastic width formula

\[
s_h(\alpha)
=
\sum_C
\frac{w_{K_C}(e_h)}D\,\alpha_C.
\]

Let

\[
a_{hC}=\frac{w_{K_C}(e_h)}D,
\qquad
a_{hC}\ge0,
\qquad
\sum_Ca_{hC}=1.
\]

The component-rotation part of an inverse blocker is

\[
\alpha_C-
\sum_{C'}a_{hC'}\alpha_{C'}.
\]

Let `S_alpha` be its row span and

\[
\boxed{
r_\alpha:=\dim\mathscr S_\alpha\le c(\Theta)-1.
}
\]

Write

\[
x_\tau=\alpha_C+\xi_\tau
\qquad(\tau\in E(C)),
\]

and quotient the blocker-row span by `S_alpha`, obtaining
\(\overline{\mathcal B}_\xi\). Then

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{rank}\mathcal B
\le
r_\alpha+
\operatorname{rank}\overline{\mathcal B}_\xi.
}
\]

Do not drop `r_alpha`, and do not count it twice.

---

# PRIMARY TARGET B

Prove the exact residual theorem

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{rank}\overline{\mathcal B}_\xi+I+r
\le
T-q-r_\alpha.
}
\]

A stronger but simpler sufficient theorem is

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{rank}\overline{\mathcal B}_\xi+I+r
\le
T-q-c(\Theta)+1.
}
\]

Either gives the total upper bound and contradicts the established lower rank by one
integer.

The proof must be componentwise:

- `s_C=2`: use both vector-closure equations and the internal turn quotient;
- cyclic `s_C=1`: use the exact two-family ordering and Ferrers staircase theorem;
- one-direction tree components: no scalar cycle obstruction;
- common component rotations: reserve exactly `r_alpha`.

The two-family numerical endpoint is already sufficient:

\[
0.8944923195\ldots D^2
<
0.9068996821\ldots D^2,
\]

with gap

\[
\boxed{0.0124073626\ldots D^2.}
\]

Do not waste the run improving this constant. The missing theorem is concentration /
rank transfer: failure of the internal coforest bound must force enough crossing mass
into one or a controlled family of two-direction components to trigger the existing
Ferrers/Cartesian contradiction.

After the pure theorem, transport it through nonquadrilateral faces, holes, multiple
components, pinches, and outside vertices with every transfer gadget charged exactly
to the topology-corrected shell residual. An uncontrolled `O(D)` loss is fatal.

