# Target A: unconditional compatible collar

Put

\[
m=N-\rho,
\qquad
d=D(m),
\qquad
t=\frac{D-d}{2},
\]

and define

\[
\boxed{
C_{\rm col}
=
\pi\left(\frac12+\frac2{\sqrt3}\right)
=
5.198395055263\ldots .
}
\]

The trusted v1.3 compatible-collar dichotomy is

\[
\boxed{
\rho<C_{\rm col}D
\quad\text{or}\quad
t>1
\text{ and a common strict sub-}D\text{ scaffold contains }
>N-C_{\rm col}D+\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt3}
\text{ points}.
}
\]

More precisely, for an independently chosen `m`-point optimum \(X_m\) with hull
\(K_m\), the safe collar

\[
\Omega
=
\operatorname{int}(K_m+t\mathbb B)
\setminus
(K_m+\mathbb B)
\]

has triangular-lattice population

\[
\boxed{
k>
\rho-C_{\rm col}D
+
\pi\left(\frac4{\sqrt3}-1\right)t
+
\pi\left(1-\frac2{\sqrt3}\right).
}
\]

In particular

\[
\boxed{
k>
ho-C_{\rm col}D+\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt3}.}
\]

Let

\[
X=X_m\cup(Y\cap\Omega).
\]

Then `X` is unit-separated and has diameter strictly less than `D`.

A surviving counterexample therefore has an exact missing-site demand

\[
\boxed{
1\le j:=N-|X|=\rho-k
<
C_{\rm col}D-\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt3}.
}
\]

This is already `O(D)`.

