# Target A: global clearance and two-well supply

For the original saturated minimizer define

\[
\delta(p)=\min_{q\ne p}|p-q|-1.
\]

The global clearance budget is

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{p\in A}\delta(p)
<
\left(24+\frac\pi2\right)D+18.
}
\]

Put

\[
L_D=
\left(24+\frac\pi2\right)D+18.
\]

If \(\rho\ge20L_D\), there is a matching `M` of strict nearest-neighbor edges, every
edge containing a deep strict rattler, with

\[
\boxed{
|M|\ge\frac\rho{24}-D-\frac34,
}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
1<|p-q|\le1+\frac{2L_D}{\rho}.
}
\]

These are strong Gabriel edges and can be included simultaneously in a Delaunay
triangulation.

The local deep-Delaunay inequality is

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{area}(T)-\frac{\sqrt3}{4}
\ge
\frac{\sqrt3}{6}\sum_{v\in T}\mu_T(v),
}
\]

with exact equality side multisets

\[
\boxed{
\{1,1,1\},
\qquad
\{1,1,\sqrt3\}.
}
\]

The global excess budget implies that along \(\rho/D\to\infty\),

\[
\boxed{
\frac\rho{24}-o(\rho)
}
\]

pairwise vertex-disjoint strong-Delaunay near-unit edges have both incident triangles
approaching one of those two equality wells.

Both wells must remain. The exact equilateral triangle is forbidden, but triangles of
side `1+epsilon` approach it quadratically while remaining triangle-free.

Do not make a `120-degree-only` argument.

