> |Q|-|N(Q)|\ge j=\rho-k.
> }
> \]
>
> That one-integer Hall surplus contradicts the exact conflict inequality and closes
> Target A.

The supply-to-demand ratio diverges:

\[
\boxed{
\frac{|M|}{j}\to\infty
}
\]

whenever `j>0` along a super-boundary sequence.

Therefore the remaining problem is not scarcity. A hypothetical survivor must somehow
make almost all of an `Omega(rho)` supply of near-equality local structures useless for
only `O(D)` insertion demand.

The most promising closing dichotomy is:

1. **near-equilateral well:** propagate into phase-coherent triangular grains; show
   triangle-freeness forces enough interfaces/defects to exceed the `O(D)` Delaunay
   excess budget or connect directly to the audited rhombic phase machinery;
2. **near-120-degree well:** propagate into ordered two-contact wedge chains; convert
   their ordering into actual independent scaffold cavities or a Hall surplus;
3. **mixed interfaces:** prove a positive finite charge per independent interface,
   again exceeding the `O(D)` budget; or
4. **alternating conflict components:** use the near-perfect conflict matching to show
   that a Hall-saturating component must carry one of the above coherent geometries.

A proof may instead discover a different exact transfer invariant. Do not feel bound
to this route if a stronger argument appears.

