# If you think Target A can now be closed by Hall alone

Be especially careful.

The exact Hall theorem already says every survivor has deficiency at most `rho-1` on
the whole lattice side and at most `j-1` on the inner band after the safe collar is
included.

To contradict it, you need an actual subset `Q` with deficiency at least `j`, or an
actual independent set of size `N`.

A heuristic statement that there are “far more candidate neighborhoods than missing
sites” is not a Hall proof.

A valid closing argument must establish simultaneous realizability or an exact
configuration-independent capacity certificate.

---

# If you think the two-well theorem closes Target A

It does not by itself.

The two-well matching lies in the original saturated minimizer. The scaffold conflict
graph lies around an independent `m`-optimum.

You need a theorem transporting geometry/capacity between them, or a global invariant
common to both.

Also retain both wells. Exact triangle-freeness does not give a positive distance from
the equilateral well.

---

# If you believe you have solved the problem

Audit only the **new closing mathematics**.

For Target A verify:

- every final point lies in the same open diameter-`D` body when claiming strict
  diameter improvement;
- every final pair is at least unit separated;
- every Hall vertex corresponds to a simultaneously realizable point or to an exact
  conflict-graph argument;
- no original Voronoi cell has been silently transported to the independent scaffold;
- no deletion-optimality assumption has re-entered;
- the near-equilateral well has not been discarded without proof;
- strict inequalities needed for `diameter < D` are preserved.

For Target B verify:

- `r_alpha` is neither dropped nor double-counted;
- the internal map acts on the actual inverse-blocker rows;
- componentwise closure dimensions are exact;
- `s_C=1` is legitimately reduced to a two-family system;
- the Cartesian block, if invoked, satisfies the audited global additive-block
  hypotheses;
- topology correction loses no uncontrolled boundary-order rank.

Run every regression test before claiming Level I.

